{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 35, :comments ["<b>One man's ox with his fellow's ox.</b> The verse's saying <b>et</b> intends that the two of them are equal in their value while alive — when they are matched against each other they are to be equal. And thus the verse's saying <b>the ox of a man et the ox</b> means: the one matched את it [i.e., against it]. But if the goring ox was larger [in value], the injured party receives only half his damages, and so it is explained in the words of our Sages of blessed memory (Bava Kamma 34a). And if the valuation of the damaging ox is not equal to that of the injured ox, one cannot say that he should take more than half the value of the living one — for if so, the verse would not have suspended the matter on the appraisal of the living ox; rather, it should have said: he shall give him half his damages from the dead one, and they shall divide the dead one. And from the fact that it is suspended on half the living ox, infer from this that he receives only half the living one, even though that may amount to less than half the damages — and it did not concern [the Torah] to err, so to speak, even if it is more than what it is worth [the reverse case]. And if you would say — follow this line: that suspending the matter on half the living one applies even when in its half there is more than half the damages, and that the verse's equation of the oxen applies even where they were not equal, so that he takes more than half — this is not so. For if you say that in a case of goring he takes half the damaging ox, you have made no distinction; if so, sometimes an ox worth two hundred will gore an ox worth fifty, and when you calculate according to this reasoning, he would receive twice his damages! And it remains to be seen what this law, as we have expounded it, is comparable to."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:32:17.199121229Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}